Therefore the equation of the circle is
\begin{align*}
(x - h)^2 + (y - k)^2 &= (h^2 - k)^2.
\end{align*}
Going back to the equation of the parabola, the equation for the circle becomes
\begin{align*}
(x - h)^2 + (x^2 - k)^2 &= (h^2 - k)^2.
\end{align*}
Equating coefficients in $x$ gives
\begin{align*}
1 + 4(x - h)(x^2 - k) &= 0.
\end{align*}
Doing a little algebra,
\begin{align*}
4(x - h)(x^2 - k) &= -1.
\end{align*}
Factoring a $(-1)$ on the left side,
\begin{align*}
(x - h)(1 - 4x^2 + 4k) &= 0.
\end{align*}
We know the bisection point is $h$, so $1 - 4x^2 + 4k = 0$ at $x = h$.  Let's call the other root of $4(x - h)(x^2 - k) = -1$ as $x = a$. 
